description:
FREESAVE, THE EXPERIMENT — doc 67 slate item G. The SAME .sav, the SAME timeline, the SAME fixture code, read by a build whose only difference is that the twelve FREE_* switches in include/config/save.h are TRUE. Both builds present themselves under the same basename on purpose: mGBA names the .sav after the ROM FILENAME, so a differently-named copy of the same build silently gets a brand-new empty save that reads exactly like a rejected one.

Each probe is at THIS build's own offsets, published by its own compiler in gFreeSaveOffsets — the point is to read the same FIELDS and watch their values change, not to read the same addresses. The two `_oldpos` probes are the exception and they are the sharpest thing here: they read where the seeded bytes USED to live, so a run can show the data still present and the field looking 156 bytes away from it.

Predictions, written before the run: status CORRUPT; party and level intact; the flags/vars fields misread while their old bytes remain; dexCaught and playTimeHours gone entirely.

TIMELINE NOTE, PAID FOR: the first version of this pair mashed A to reach CONTINUE. On the flipped build that mash walked PAST the corrupt-save message and committed to NEW GAME, whose NewGameInitData() zeroes every block -- so all ten probes read 0 and it looked exactly like 'the load discarded everything'. The screenshot showed Birch mid-speech and settled it in one look. Doc 69's rule, ignored by the person who wrote it down: when a mash must cross a screen that becomes interactive, use a button that cannot commit -- or, as here, press nothing. The save is loaded by Save_LoadGameData during the COPYRIGHT SCREEN (doc 61 §3), long before the menu exists, so no input is needed to observe it and any input is a risk. Both arms now run the identical no-input timeline.

PREDICTION FALSIFIED, AND THIS IS THE RESULT OF THE ROUND. The prediction above said the loud failure and the silent misread would happen in the SAME load: sectors 1-3 keep their size, so they keep passing their checksum, and CopySaveSlotData is called UNCONDITIONALLY by TryLoadSaveSlot regardless of the status. All of that is true, and it does not matter. Five lines after the load, at src/intro.c:1165, CB2_InitCopyrightScreenAfterBootup does:
    LoadGameSave(SAVE_NORMAL);
    if (gSaveFileStatus == SAVE_STATUS_EMPTY || gSaveFileStatus == SAVE_STATUS_CORRUPT)
        Sav2_ClearSetDefault();
so the partially-copied data survives for a handful of instructions and is then erased at the same call site. CORRUPT is handled IDENTICALLY TO EMPTY. The engine's response to a size-changing save-layout edit is therefore total and immediate, not partial -- which is better behaviour than predicted, and it is why this arm reads zero everywhere. The reading that produced the wrong prediction stopped at TryLoadSaveSlot and never asked who called it.

CONSEQUENCE FOR THE SLATE: the FREE_* switches can NEVER demonstrate doc 41's silent-misread class, because every one of them changes a struct size and the engine catches every size change. Showing that class needs a SIZE-PRESERVING layout edit, which is freesave-read-swapped.json.
